Apple acquired AI audio editing app Samplab in June, according to a <a href="https://digital-markets-act-cases.ec.europa.eu/acquisitions">notice shared today</a> by the European Commission.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/samplab.jpg" alt="" width="1280" height="720" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1071689" /><br/>
Samplab offered a desktop app and plugins for converting audio to MIDI, detecting and editing chords, stem splitting for isolating different parts of a song, and editing notes in polyphonic audio without affecting the timbre of the original.<br/>
<br/>
Apple could fold the company's tools into Logic Pro or GarageBand. Logic Pro already has Stem Splitter and Chord ID, but polyphonic note editing would be new.<br/>
<br/>
The filing says Apple acquired "certain assets" from Samplab and hired "certain Samplab employees." Apple is considered a "gatekeeper" in the European Union, and the Digital Markets Act requires it to inform the EC about deals involving core platform services, digital services, and data collection.<br/>
<br/>
